• Maintain, preserve, and extend the life and utility of prior investments in transportation systems and
services;
• Pullman Transit assesses its schedules and service area coverage to match public need, by means of
transparent public engagement whether by phone, email, mobile app, or at public meetings;
• Route service schedules will continue to receive adjustments to meet rider needs, and improve
coordination between existing routes (including the continued consideration of line/loop/transfer
alternatives) for efficiency and effectiveness;
• Dial-A-Ride services will continue to meet the requirements of the Americans with Disabilities Act;
• Pullman Transit will continue to promote regional connection information with four other public
transportation providers, as well as interstate bus and private carriers;
• Pullman Transit will research future opportunities for greater connectivity to the South Eastern region of
Washington State;
• Pullman Transit will research future opportunities for greater connectivity with the region’s economically
disadvantaged, those needing medical transportation and Veteran transportation as the opportunities
and funding arise;
• Pullman Transit will continue to coordinate with local law enforcement agencies and emergency
services, both with the City of Pullman and with WSU;
• Staff will continue their active participation in local and regional planning efforts including the Palouse
Regional Transportation Planning Organization;
• Pullman Transit will continue to research and monitor opportunities for bus service to Moscow, Idaho,
with a focus on economic sustainability of the expansion. This effort will include identifying logistical and
financial obstacles for providing this connection as a fixed route service;
• Use increasingly accurate AVL/GPS data to modify service delivery model, reflecting identified peak
time and location needs that could require additional or more frequent vehicles.
• Continue a commitment to pickup and dropoff locations often used by members of the community in
need, regardless of data showing low or infrequent ridership.
• Utilize internal local government resources to formalize transit planning efforts into three, five and ten
year transit service planning models.

• Preserve existing public transportation facilities and equipment.
• Pullman Transit will continue to replace vehicles in accordance with the adopted fleet replacement
standards identified in the Transit Asset Management Plan (TAMP) and scheduled in the system’s
Capital Improvement Program (CIP);
• Pullman Transit will continue to research Electric and other clean fuel equipment options reduce both
noise and air pollution in the areas of transit operation;
• Pullman Transit will continue to follow and research the latest developments of the State Clean Air Act as
well as the Alternative Fuels Act as mandated by the State of Washington;
• Pullman Transit will continue to add and improve shelter locations, trash receptacles, landscaping, and
cleanliness on buses and at the Transfer Station. This will include evaluating ridership data and public
input, then adding shelters at bus stops where warranted;
• Pullman Transit will consider fixed route vehicle options smaller than 35 and 40-foot vehicles, if the lesser
capacity and maneuverability will be beneficial to service delivery model;
• As service expands, Pullman Transit will consider whether the current Davis Way transfer station meets
the system needs, or if an alternate location would better serve the system;
• Staff will continue to work with consultants to design main office/garage facility expansion options, then
seek funding opportunities to complete.

